+{"q": 117, "question": "Who is the most likely murderer?", "narrative": "In the heart of the rainforest, Jesse meets his gruesome end by a lawnmower, leaving Detective Winston embarked on a puzzling quest with suspects - Sam and Steve.\n\nDetective Winston stood at the edge of the rainforest, reviewing his notes. The scene was grim, even for a seasoned detective. The body of Jesse, a young boy, had been found. The murder weapon, found near his body, was a lawnmower, an odd choice for a murder weapon, especially in a rainforest.\n\nOne suspect was Jesse's fellow schoolmate, Sam. His name had surfaced around the investigation thanks to a tip-off from the school janitor. The man remembered seeing Sam at the lost and found desk, writing something.\n\nIt appears that Sam had found some incriminating texts on his girlfriend's phone, all of which were sent by Jesse. When confronted, Sam's girlfriend confessed that she had been seeing Jesse behind Sam's back. This all coincided with the fact that Sam had access to paper and pen during his visit to the lost and found desk. Winston also learned that the numbers from the confiscated phones were noted down on a piece of paper.\n\nThe field trip group Sam and Jesse were part of were the only ones in the rainforest that day. Sam was among them, having also been given special privileges, being the only student allowed to access the lost and found box. Winston made yet another note of it. \n\n\"Winston,\" a fellow detective called out, causing Winston to look up. \n\n\"What is it?\" He asked, heading towards the man.\n\n\"Just wanted you to know, we've seized the phones. The numbers were written down.\"\n\n\"On paper?\"\n\nThe detective nodded, \"Yeah. At the lost and found desk, from what we've heard.\"\n\nWinston's eyes squinted as he thought. He knew these facts were significant, but their connection was not clear. \n\n\"Okay. Thanks.\" Giving him a nod, he moved towards his vehicle. \n\nHis mind whirled with facts as he starte