d himself. \n\n\"Salvador?\" \n\n\"Yeah, that's me,\" he replied, wiping sweat from his brow.\n\nWinston motioned to a nearby bench. \"Do you mind if we chat for a few moments?\"\n\nSalvador glanced at his watch. \"Sure, I've got a few minutes.\"\n\nThey sat. \n\n\"Nice day out, isn't it?\" Canvasing the area, Winston's eyes landed on a small poster. The image was of a verdant campground, similar to the one where Hugo was killed. \"You like camping, Salvador?\"\n\nSalvador, caught off guard, blinked. \"Yes, I do. I go regularly, whenever I can... even to that campground over there\". \n\nThey chatted about the great outdoors, the joy of being connected with nature, and the complexities of their camping hobbies. Salvador didn\u2019t seem aware that Winston\u2019s questions were leading to anything in particular.\n\nAfter a few minutes of casual conversation, Winston stood up from the bench. \"Thanks for talking with me, Salvador. Nice day, isn't it?\" \n\n\"Yes, indeed,\" Salvador replied, stretching his arms. He watched Winston drove away curiously, before shaking his head and walking back to his car.\n\nWinston's next stop was Salvador's house; he observed from a safe distance as Salvador fiddled with something in his garage. Later, when Salvador had retreated into his house, Winston took a closer look under the cover of darkness. He could see an array of axes, various sizes and shapes, displayed proudly on a wall. He saw newly chopped wood stacked neatly in the corner of his backyard, evidence of Salvador\u2019s recent chopping endeavours.\n\nWinston shared his observations with the local police but stepped back after providing his input. They would carry out any necessary searches with legal authorisations while Winston handled the interviews, suspicions and evidence collecting. It was a symbiotic relationship of professional respect.\n\nThe puzzling thing nagging at Winston's mind was Hugo's recent decision to stop supplying Salvador with drugs. Something provok